          }
        },
        "etag" : "\"Q44G99shyMl14lSVN9oTpl4EamI/4grJbc19eakWD8YtMnxmBTe6554\"",
        "id" : "z12acbjatynfz5wbu04cexrqywbvcrqgizk0k.1410452577338201",
        "kind" : "plus#comment",
        "object" : {
          "content" : "<span class=\"proflinkWrapper\"><span class=\"proflinkPrefix\">+</span><a class=\"proflink\" href=\"https://plus.google.com/107457342204653676533\" oid=\"107457342204653676533\">Dillon Nichols</a></span> Great question.  The &quot;Windows on Devices&quot; is in &quot;beta&quot; phase if you will. The current Windows release is just a first pass.  The team is actively working on updated versions of windows for devices, as well as already supporting more boards than just the Galileo (like the minnowboard max, and the Sharks Cove hardware dev boards).  The result is that existing Win32 as well as .NET devs will have the ability to create applications that run across a variety of devices, not just on an Arduino, or a Pi.  The steps I walk through here are to help early adopters use the current release of Windows for devices, but the OS, tool chain, and language support should expand greatly in the future. ",
          "objectType" : "comment"
        },
